+  conflict_keys:
+    production:
+    - Executor.preStateCompensations
+    - HomeLock
+    tla: []
+    adapters: []
+    test_graph: []
+    workflow_data: []
+  found_by: revision-4 sweep of open issues against the owner's all-home-bugs instruction
+  root_cause: 'Executor.java:307 -- `case SkillEffect.CommitUnitsToStore e -> List.of();` An EMPTY pre-state
+    compensation, in an exhaustive switch, for the one effect that overwrites bytes already in the home.
+    The commit deletes the destination and copies the new tree in; the walk-back then deletes what it
+    finds and restores nothing, because nothing was captured. A publish or resolve that fails partway
+    into a NON-EMPTY home therefore leaves that unit absent rather than as it was. The severity scales
+    with the tier: root is the home with the most installed units and the least test coverage.
+
+    Second half, from the same issue: no HomeLock on the resolve/install path, so two concurrent resolves
+    interleave staging and commit. That is squarely per-home isolation and belongs with the first half
+    rather than after it.
+
+    '
+  slice: 'Capture the pre-image before the destructive commit and restore it on walk-back; take the home
+    lock for the resolve/install path. NOT IN SCOPE: any other effect''s compensation, and the compensation
+    FRAMEWORK''s shape.
+
+    '
+  acceptance:
+    assertions:
+    - A resolve that fails after CommitUnitsToStore leaves every previously installed unit byte-identical
+      to what it was.
+    - That assertion FAILS when the move-aside is removed -- vacuity checked, in writing.
+    - Two concurrent resolves into one home do not interleave; the second waits or refuses, and says which.
+    - The pre-image is cleaned up on success, so the guard does not grow the home without bound.
+  goals:
+  - goal: GOAL-no-destructive-recovery
+    contribution: direct
+    expected_effect: 0 pre-existing unit bytes survive a failed install -> all of them survive
+    local_signal: jbang RunTests.java
+- id: HIS-12
+  title: a refusal names a home you were not in and a build that cannot run the remedy, and project sync
+    loses the typed refusal entirely
