+  conflict_keys:
+    production:
+    - HomeDescriptor.resolveCli
+    - HomeDescriptor.cliSpelling
+    - ProjectCommand.SyncCmd
+    tla: []
+    adapters: []
+    test_graph: []
+    workflow_data: []
+  found_by: '#161 and #187 from the revision-4 sweep, joined to DEF-002 from this epic''s own kickoff
+    -- three faces of one surface, the refusal a home operation prints.
+
+    '
+  root_cause: 'A REMEDY IS AN INSTRUCTION, AND THESE ONES EDIT A DIFFERENT HOME THAN THE ONE YOU WERE
+    IN.
+
+    HomeDescriptor.resolveCli (:379-395) falls through to `onPath( "skill-manager")` with no version check,
+    so the printed remedy names an absolute path to whatever build PATH happened to find -- often an older
+    release that cannot run the remedy it is being named in. Step 2 of that same precedence (the current
+    process) is DEAD in every shipped launcher, because the process basename is jbang or java, so the
+    javadoc''s stated precedence is false as well.
+
+    DEF-002 is the same defect landing on the operator: a sync run against the PROJECT home prints `re-run
+    with: /Users/hayde/.skill-manager/bin/cli/skill-manager sync <unit> --merge`. Following that verbatim
+    edits the ROOT home.
+
+    #187 is the same surface losing its type: ProjectCommand.ResolveCmd catches ProjectImportViolationException
+    (:166) and renders it; SyncCmd does not, so the identical failure arrives as an untyped IOException
+    with a generic exit.
+
+    '
+  slice: 'Make the refusal name the home it is about and a build that can run it: mark the PATH-fallback
+    case rather than presenting it as authoritative, correct the javadoc''s precedence, spell the remedy
+    against the home the command was given, and give SyncCmd the typed catch ResolveCmd already has. NOT
+    IN SCOPE: changing what any of those operations DO.
+
+    '
+  acceptance:
+    assertions:
+    - A remedy printed by a command run against home X names X, never another home.
+    - A remedy resolved by the PATH fallback says so, rather than reading as authoritative.
+    - '`project sync` renders a ProjectImportViolationException with the same typed output and exit code
+      as `project resolve`.'
+    - The javadoc's stated precedence matches what the code does, proved by a test that walks it.
+  goals:
+  - goal: GOAL-one-home-one-answer
+    contribution: direct
+    expected_effect: 'the remedy surface joins the readers that agree: a printed command names the home
+      the operator was in
+
+      '
+    local_signal: jbang RunTests.java
